<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<Shell
    x:Class="KeyKeeper.AppShell"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
    xmlns:views="clr-namespace:KeyKeeper.Views"
    FlyoutBehavior="Disabled"
    Title="KeyKeeper">

    <!--
    Four top-level destinations, each its own implicit ShellItem/ShellSection/ShellContent
    (skipping explicit ShellItem/ShellSection tags is fine — Shell auto-wraps a bare ShellContent
    in one of each). That's deliberate, not just the terse option: an absolute GoToAsync("//x")
    call requires its target to be reachable as a full page stack on its own, which only a real
    ShellContent in this visual hierarchy provides — a route registered purely via
    Routing.RegisterRoute (no ShellContent) can only be pushed relatively onto an existing stack,
    not navigated to absolutely as the entire stack. (Learned the hard way: the initial version
    of this file declared only "lock" here and registered "setup"/"vault" as RegisterRoute-only
    global routes, which crashed on startup with "Global routes currently cannot be the only page
    on the stack" the moment App.xaml.cs tried GoToAsync("//setup").) "security-warning" (Phase 7)
    followed this same rule from the start rather than repeating that mistake — see
    IDeviceSecurityWarningService: App.xaml.cs can navigate to it absolutely, before any other page
    exists yet, right after the vault-security check runs at launch.

    FlyoutBehavior="Disabled" hides the flyout hamburger that four top-level ShellItems would
    otherwise imply; no tab bar appears either, since tabs are a ShellSection-level construct
    (multiple ShellContent under one ShellSection), not something four separate ShellItems
    trigger. App.xaml.cs decides which of the four is actually shown, immediately on startup —
    see its CreateWindow override.

    "setup" being declared FIRST matters here, unlike the other three: Shell instantiates its
    first-declared ShellContent as its own implicit default the moment AppShell() runs, firing
    that page's OnAppearing before App.xaml.cs's CreateWindow/NavigateToInitialRouteAsync has run
    at all. Declaring "security-warning" first (tried during this phase's own manual verification)
    made *it* that implicit default instead: SecurityWarningPage.OnAppearing called
    SecurityWarningViewModel.Initialize() while IDeviceSecurityWarningService.PendingWarning was
    still null (EvaluateDeviceSecurity hadn't run yet), leaving Message permanently blank, since
    App.xaml.cs's later GoToAsync("//security-warning") is a same-route no-op once Shell already
    considers itself there, so OnAppearing never re-fires to pick up the real PendingWarning. Kept
    "setup" first as it always was; "security-warning" only needs to be *reachable* as a real
    ShellContent (per the routing lesson above), not the default one.
    -->
    <ShellContent
        Route="setup"
        ContentTemplate="{DataTemplate views:SetupPage}" />

    <ShellContent
        Route="lock"
        ContentTemplate="{DataTemplate views:LockPage}" />

    <ShellContent
        Route="vault"
        ContentTemplate="{DataTemplate views:VaultListPage}" />

    <ShellContent
        Route="security-warning"
        ContentTemplate="{DataTemplate views:SecurityWarningPage}" />

    <!--
    The five item-type form pages (Phase 4) are deliberately NOT declared as ShellContent here —
    unlike "setup"/"lock"/"vault" above, nothing ever navigates to them absolutely (GoToAsync
    "//login-form" etc.); they're only ever pushed relatively on top of "vault"
    (GoToAsync("login-form") or ".../login-form?id=...") from VaultListViewModel, so the
    RegisterRoute-only global-route form is exactly right for them — see AppShell.xaml.cs.
    -->

</Shell>
